/** \addtogroup area_sequence */
/** \defgroup theory_sequence Theory of sequences and lists
 \ingroup area_sequence

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/sequence` realize the SEQUENCE of
 section 3.2.1 of the book and the LIST of chapter 4, i.e., a vector that
 doubles and halves (section 4.2), the bidirectional circular list with a
 sentinel (section 4.1), the singly linked list of exercise 4.6, the lists
 with cursors in one vector, and, beyond the book, a vector with room for a
 few items inside itself and the tiered vector of Goodrich and Kloss; next to
 them are the algorithms that work on a sequence through its positions (the
 searches, the natural merge sort, the merge) or on a vector in place (the
 partition into three classes and a few other rearrangements). This page
 collects the definitions and the notation that the headers share (see
 \ref theory_sequence_notation), the realizations with the costs of their
 operations (see \ref theory_sequence_repr), the amortized analysis of the
 vectors that change their size (see \ref theory_sequence_amortized), and,
 for the algorithms, the property on which each is correct and the argument
 behind its bound (see \ref theory_sequence_sort and
 \ref theory_sequence_rearr). The pseudocode of each operation and of each
 algorithm is on the page of its header, in the comment of its function,
 together with the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_sequence_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>sequence</em> \f$ s = s_0 , s_1 , \ldots , s_{n-1} \f$ is a finite
 list of \f$ n \ge 0 \f$ items, possibly repeated, in which the order matters;
 the SEQUENCE of the book (see \ref theory_ADS_adt, and the concept
 `Sequence` of `ADT.h`) reaches its items through <em>positions</em>, values
 of a type `Pos` that the realization chooses, rather than through indices.
 The position \f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$ leads to \f$ s_i \f$ for
 \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$, and two more positions, \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$ and
 \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$, stand before the first item and after the last one,
 so that a scan reads
 \f[
   p \gets \mathrm{head}();\ \textbf{while not}\ \mathrm{finished}( p )
   \ \textbf{do}\ \ldots\ \mathrm{read}( p ) \ldots ;\ p \gets \mathrm{next}( p
   ) . \tag{1}
 \f]
 What a position is decides the cost of each operation: an index costs
 nothing to move along but makes an insertion shift the items after it,
 while a pointer to a cell makes an insertion change two links but gives no
 access by index. A <em>run</em> of a sequence is a maximal stretch of
 consecutive items in nondecreasing order, and a sequence of \f$ r \f$ runs
 has \f$ r - 1 \f$ descents, i.e., places with \f$ s_{i+1} < s_i \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ s_i \f$</td>
     <td>the number of items, and the item of index \f$ i \f$,
         \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$</td>
     <td>the position of \f$ s_i \f$, and \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$,
         \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ those before the first item and after the
         last one, on which <tt>finished()</tt> is true</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of a vector, the number of items it has room
         for</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha = n / c \f$</td>
     <td>the load of a vector</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \Phi \f$</td>
     <td>a potential, a function of the state of a structure that pays for
         its dear operations</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ b \f$</td>
     <td>the number of places of a block of the tiered vector, a power of
         2</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ r \f$</td>
     <td>the number of runs of a sequence</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$</td>
     <td>a vector of the C++ code, indexed from 0, and \f$ A[ i .. j ) \f$
         its places from \f$ i \f$ to \f$ j - 1 \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_sequence_repr Realizations and their costs

 The library has six realizations of a sequence, four of which have the
 positions of the book and model `Sequence`, while the other two are vectors
 with access by index only.

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of a sequence and the cost of their operations
          (sections 4.1 and 4.2 of the book, exercise 4.6)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Position</th><th>Space</th>
     <th><tt>read()</tt>, <tt>next()</tt></th><th><tt>prev()</tt>,
     <tt>tail()</tt></th><th><tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt></th>
     <th>access by index</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArraySequence</tt></td><td>the index</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n - i ) \f$, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
     amortized at the end</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>List</tt></td><td>the cell of the item</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( i ) \f$ by a scan</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SinglyList</tt></td><td>the cell before the item</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( i ) \f$ by a scan</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CursorList</tt></td><td>the cursor of the cell before the
     item</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, shared by the lists of a space</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ O( i ) \f$ by a scan</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SmallVector</tt></td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + N ) \f$</td><td>none</td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized at the end</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TieredVector</tt></td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ n + O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$</td><td>none</td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$ amortized anywhere</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 An insertion in a vector at the index \f$ i \f$ moves the \f$ n - i \f$
 items after it one place on, and a removal moves them back; hence the
 vector is preferable when the sequence is mostly read, by index or in
 order, and changed at its end, and its items are contiguous, which makes a
 scan read the memory forward. A list changes in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time at any
 position it holds, since an insertion allocates a cell and changes the
 links of its two neighbors, but it has no access by index, and a scan
 follows pointers that may be scattered in memory; the cells of `List` and
 of `SinglyList` come therefore from a pool, so that the cells made one
 after another are next to each other.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the position before the item).</em> In a list whose cells
 have a link to the next one only, an insertion before \f$ s_i \f$ and the
 removal of \f$ s_i \f$ change the link of the cell of \f$ s_{i-1} \f$ (or
 the link to the first cell, for \f$ i = 0 \f$); with
 \f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$ the cell of \f$ s_{i-1} \f$, and nil for
 \f$ i = 0 \f$, both take \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, while `prev()` and `tail()`
 take \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst case.

 Indeed, the link to change is the one that points to the cell of
 \f$ s_i \f$, which is in the cell before it, and no link leads from a cell
 back to its predecessor; a position that held the cell of \f$ s_i \f$ itself
 would force a scan from the head to find it, i.e., \f$ \Theta( i ) \f$
 time. With the position before the item, the scan is needed only to go
 backwards, i.e., by `prev()` and by `tail()`, which is the convention of the
 realizations with pointers and with cursors of Aho, Hopcroft and Ullman.
 `SinglyList` and `CursorList` follow it, with \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ the
 last cell; after `insert( p , v )` the new item has the position \f$ p \f$,
 and after `remove( p )` its successor has it. The bidirectional list
 `List` keeps instead both links in each cell and a sentinel, a cell with no
 item that is the predecessor of the first cell and the successor of the
 last one: every cell has then both neighbors, and the insertion and the
 removal have no special case for the empty list or for the ends, the
 sentinel being \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$ and \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ at once.

 A list with <em>cursors</em> (`CursorList`) keeps its cells in a vector,
 shared by several lists, the link of a cell being the index of the next one
 and 0 being nil; the free cells form one more list in the same vector, the
 free list, from which `take()` removes its first cell and to which `give()`
 returns a cell, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time. When the free list is empty the
 vector doubles, which costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized by Lemma 2 below, and
 the cursors stay valid, since an index does not change when the vector is
 moved, while a pointer would.

 \section theory_sequence_amortized Vectors that change their size

 A vector of capacity \f$ c \f$ holds \f$ n \le c \f$ items; when an
 insertion finds it full it is replaced by one of capacity \f$ 2 c \f$, and
 the \f$ n \f$ items are moved there, which costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$. The
 cost of a single insertion is therefore \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst
 case, but that of a sequence of operations is linear in their number.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the doubling, section 4.2.1).</em> Starting from the empty
 vector, \f$ n \f$ insertions at the end cost in all
 \f[
   C( n ) = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \le n + \sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor}
            2^k \le n + 2^{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1} - 1 \le 3 n ,
   \tag{2}
 \f]
 with \f$ c_i = i \f$ when the \f$ i \f$-th insertion finds the vector full,
 i.e., when \f$ i - 1 \f$ is a power of 2, and \f$ c_i = 1 \f$ otherwise.

 The bound (2) is the sum of the moves of the doublings, each a power of 2
 up to \f$ n \f$, and of one write per insertion; hence an insertion at the
 end costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized. Growing the vector by a constant
 \f$ d \f$ instead of doubling it gives a resize every \f$ d \f$
 insertions, the \f$ k \f$-th one moving \f$ k d \f$ items, i.e.,
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 / d ) \f$ in all, which is quadratic.

 A vector that only grows keeps the space of its largest size. To keep the
 space \f$ O( n ) \f$, `ArraySequence` halves its capacity when a removal
 leaves it with \f$ n \le c / 4 \f$ items (halving it at \f$ n = c / 2 \f$
 would make an insertion and a removal around that size each cost
 \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, a resize every time).

 <em>Lemma 3 (the doubling and the halving).</em> Any sequence of insertions
 and removals at the end of a vector that doubles when it is full and halves
 when it is a quarter full costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized time per operation.

 Indeed, let the potential be
 \f[
   \Phi = \begin{cases} 2 n - c & \text{if } \alpha \ge 1 / 2 , \cr
                        c / 2 - n & \text{if } \alpha < 1 / 2 ,
          \end{cases} \tag{3}
 \f]
 which is never negative and is 0 when the vector is half full. An operation
 that does not resize changes \f$ n \f$ by 1 and \f$ \Phi \f$ by at most 2,
 i.e., its amortized cost, its cost plus the change of \f$ \Phi \f$, is at
 most 3. A doubling happens at \f$ n = c \f$, where \f$ \Phi = n \f$, and a
 halving at \f$ n = c / 4 \f$, where \f$ \Phi = c / 4 = n \f$: in both cases
 the \f$ n \f$ moves are paid by the potential, which falls to \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 (Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest and Stein, section 17.4, after Tarjan).
 `CursorSpace` grows in the same way, and `SmallVector` doubles once its
 \f$ N \f$ inner places are full, so that \f$ n \f$ insertions move fewer than
 \f$ 2 n \f$ items and the space is at most \f$ 2 n + N \f$ items.

 The tiered vector of Goodrich and Kloss trades the access in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ of a vector for an insertion and a removal at any index in
 \f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$. Its items are in blocks of \f$ b \f$ places, each a
 circular buffer with the offset of its first item, all full but the last
 one; the item of index \f$ i \f$ is the item \f$ i \bmod b \f$ of the block
 \f$ \lfloor i / b \rfloor \f$, i.e., with \f$ b \f$ a power of 2, a shift
 and a mask, and inside the block it is at the place
 \f$ ( \mathit{off} + i ) \bmod b \f$.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the tiered vector).</em> An insertion or a removal at the index
 \f$ i \f$ moves \f$ O( b + n / b ) \f$ items, i.e., \f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$
 with \f$ b = \Theta( \sqrt{n} ) \f$, and keeping \f$ b \f$ the least power of
 2 not below \f$ \sqrt{n} \f$, by a rebuild in \f$ O( n ) \f$ when \f$ n \f$
 leaves \f$ [ b^2 / 4 , 4 b^2 ] \f$, costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized per
 operation.

 Indeed, an insertion moves by one place the items of its block after
 \f$ i \f$, fewer than \f$ b \f$, and then, from the last block back to the
 block after that of \f$ i \f$, the last item of each block to the front of
 the next one; the front of a circular buffer moves by decreasing its
 offset, i.e., in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and there are at most \f$ n / b + 1 \f$
 blocks. A removal does the converse. Right after a rebuild \f$ b \f$ is
 the least power of 2 with \f$ b^2 \ge n \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ b^2 / 4 < n \le b^2 \f$, and the rebuilds that \f$ n \f$ crossing a
 bound triggers leave \f$ n \f$ about \f$ b^2 \f$ for the new \f$ b \f$; the
 next rebuild needs \f$ n \f$ to grow to \f$ 4 b^2 \f$ or to fall to
 \f$ b^2 / 4 \f$, i.e., \f$ \Omega( n ) \f$ operations, which pay for its
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ moves. The space is that of the \f$ n \f$ items and of at
 most one block not full, i.e., \f$ n + O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$.

 \section theory_sequence_sort Searching, merging and sorting a sequence

 The algorithms of `SequenceAlgorithms.h` use only the operations of the
 SEQUENCE, so that they run on every realization, with the cost of a scan
 (1), i.e., \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ calls of `next()` and `read()`. A search for
 an item compares it with the items in order, \f$ n \f$ comparisons when it
 misses; on a sorted sequence it may stop at the first larger item, but
 the comparisons stay \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst case, since the
 positions give no access in the middle (the binary search of the vectors
 needs the access by index).
 The merge of two sorted sequences of lengths \f$ n_1 \f$ and \f$ n_2 \f$
 appends at each step the smaller of their first items not yet taken, the
 one of the first sequence among equals, i.e., it is stable and makes at most
 \f$ n_1 + n_2 - 1 \f$ comparisons.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the natural merge sort).</em> A pass that distributes the
 runs of a sequence alternately to two sequences and merges them in pairs
 leaves at most \f$ \lceil r / 2 \rceil \f$ runs; hence the sort of a
 sequence of \f$ r \f$ runs makes \f$ \lceil \log_2 r \rceil \f$ passes and
 costs \f$ O( n \log r ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ in the worst case
 and \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ on a sorted sequence, which one scan finds to be a
 single run.

 Indeed, the merge of two sorted runs is a sorted stretch, and two runs that
 go to the same sequence one after another may only join into one; the
 \f$ j \f$-th pair of the merge therefore covers the runs \f$ 2 j - 1 \f$ and
 \f$ 2 j \f$ of the pass, and gives one run or part of one. Each pass costs
 \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ reads and writes. The bound adapts to the order already
 present, as \f$ \log r \f$ falls from \f$ \log n \f$ to 0. It is also
 optimal up to a constant factor in the worst case for a given \f$ r \f$:
 the permutations whose runs have the lengths
 \f$ \ell_1 , \ldots , \ell_r \f$ are at most
 \f$ n! / ( \ell_1! \cdots \ell_r! ) \f$, whose logarithm is at most
 \f$ n \log_2 r \f$ and is \f$ \Omega( n \log r ) \f$ when the runs are
 of equal lengths, and a sort by comparisons must tell them apart (McIlroy).
 The bubble sort, instead, swaps
 adjacent items out of order until a pass has no swap; each swap removes one
 inversion, and the passes are at most \f$ n \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ comparisons and swaps.

 `listSort()` of `ListSort.h` sorts a `List` by relinking its cells, so
 that no item is copied and the positions stay on their items. It cuts the
 list into its runs, reversing those strictly decreasing (strictly, so that
 the sort stays stable), and merges them as a binary counter: the place
 \f$ k \f$ of an array holds the merge of \f$ 2^k \f$ runs or nothing, and a
 new run is merged with the places \f$ 0 , 1 , \ldots \f$ while they are
 full, as a carry propagates. Each run takes part in at most
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 r \rceil + 1 \f$ merges, which gives
 \f$ O( n \log r ) \f$ comparisons and \f$ n - 1 \f$ on a sorted or a
 reversed list, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ space beyond the array of 64 places. On
 the numbers in their natural order it uses instead the radix sort on the
 pairs (key, cell), in \f$ O( n ) \f$ time and space, since a merge sort
 walks the scattered cells \f$ \log r \f$ times and misses the cache at each
 of them.

 \section theory_sequence_rearr Rearrangements in place

 The functions of `Rearrangements.h` reorder a vector \f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$
 by swaps, or with a buffer much smaller than \f$ n \f$.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the Dutch national flag, Dijkstra).</em> With the indices
 \f$ i \le j \le k \f$, the invariant
 \f[
   A[ 0 .. i ) \text{ of color 0} , \quad A[ i .. j ) \text{ of color 1} ,
   \quad A[ j .. k ) \text{ not examined} , \quad A[ k .. n )
   \text{ of color 2} \tag{4}
 \f]
 holds at the start with \f$ i = j = 0 \f$ and \f$ k = n \f$ and is kept by
 a step that examines \f$ A[ j ] \f$; hence the items are partitioned by
 color after \f$ n \f$ steps, with \f$ n \f$ reads of a color and at most
 \f$ n \f$ swaps.

 Indeed, an item of color 0 is swapped with \f$ A[ i ] \f$, which is of
 color 1 (or is \f$ A[ j ] \f$ itself), and both \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$
 grow; one of color 1 makes \f$ j \f$ grow; one of color 2 is swapped with
 \f$ A[ k - 1 ] \f$, not examined yet, and \f$ k \f$ falls. Each step makes
 the stretch not examined one shorter, and swaps at most once. It is the
 partition of the quicksort with three ways, the keys smaller than, equal to
 and larger than the pivot being the three colors.

 The alternating order \f$ A[ 0 ] \le A[ 1 ] \ge A[ 2 ] \le A[ 3 ] \ge \cdots
 \f$ takes one pass of \f$ n - 1 \f$ comparisons (`wiggleSort()`): if
 \f$ A[ 0 .. i ] \f$ alternates and \f$ A[ i ] \f$ and \f$ A[ i + 1 ] \f$ are
 in the wrong order, their swap keeps the order of \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \f$ and
 \f$ A[ i ] \f$, by transitivity (e.g., for an odd \f$ i \f$ with
 \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \le A[ i ] < A[ i + 1 ] \f$, the swap puts at the place
 \f$ i \f$ the larger \f$ A[ i + 1 ] \f$, which is still at least
 \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \f$).

 A vector is \f$ k \f$-<em>sorted</em> when each item is at most \f$ k \f$
 places away from its place in the sorted order; then the item of the place
 \f$ i \f$ of the sorted order is the least of those left among
 \f$ A[ i .. i + k ] \f$, and `sortKSorted()` keeps them in a heap of
 \f$ k + 1 \f$ items, which gives each item in \f$ O( \log k ) \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n \log k ) \f$ in all against the \f$ O( n k ) \f$ of the insertion
 sort. No sort by comparisons does better: the \f$ k \f$-sorted orders of
 \f$ n \f$ items are at least \f$ ( k! )^{n / k} \f$ (the blocks of \f$ k \f$
 places permuted independently), and a decision tree with that many leaves
 has a height of at least
 \f$ \log_2 ( k! )^{n / k} = \Omega( n \log k ) \f$.

 Finally, the permutation that sorts distinct items splits into cycles, and
 a swap that puts one item in its place shortens its cycle by one; a cycle
 of \f$ k > 1 \f$ items needs therefore \f$ k - 1 \f$ swaps, and the fewest
 swaps that sort the vector are \f$ n \f$ minus the number of cycles
 (`minimumSwapSort()`). When a swap costs the sum of its two items, a cycle
 of \f$ k \f$ items of sum \f$ s \f$ and least item \f$ m \f$ is sorted at
 the least cost
 \f[
   \min\{ s + ( k - 2 ) m , \ s + m + ( k + 1 ) M \} , \tag{5}
 \f]
 \f$ M \f$ the least item of the vector: either \f$ m \f$ is swapped in turn
 with each other item of the cycle, which takes part in one swap while
 \f$ m \f$ takes part in \f$ k - 1 \f$, or \f$ M \f$ enters the cycle by a
 swap with \f$ m \f$, does the same \f$ k - 1 \f$ swaps, and is swapped back
 (Knuth, section 1.3.3, on the cycles of a permutation).

